  <article>
    <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href="/learn">Learn</a> / Break into tech without a CS degree</nav>
    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>How to break into tech without a CS degree</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">You break into tech without a CS degree by proving you can do the work, role by role. Most tech jobs never required computer science in the first place, and the ones that do increasingly weigh demonstrated projects over the diploma. The move is to pick a role, produce real work in it, and get that work evaluated and seen by recruiters.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>Most tech roles, including product, design, marketing, data, sales, and operations, do not require a CS degree.</li>
        <li>Even in engineering, evidence of skill matters more as <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers use skills-based hiring</a> (TestGorilla, 2025).</li>
        <li>The credential was always a proxy for ability. Tech, more than most industries, is willing to look at the ability directly.</li>
        <li>Bootcamps and courses can teach, but a completion certificate is not proof. Favor anything that produces evaluated, recruiter-visible work.</li>
        <li>The fastest route is to build a few scored work samples in your target role, not to collect another credential.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Do you actually need a CS degree to work in tech?</h2>
      <p>For most of tech, no, and the belief that you do is one of the most expensive misunderstandings a student can carry. A CS degree trains you to build software, which matters for software engineering. But a technology company is full of roles that are not software engineering: the people who decide what to build, who design how it works, who bring it to market, who analyze whether it is working, who sell it and support it. None of those were ever gated by a CS degree. They are gated by judgment, communication, and the ability to do the work, which is exactly what you can demonstrate without a specific diploma.</p>
      <p>Even for engineering itself, the gate is loosening. The degree was a proxy, a way for employers to guess at coding ability they could not see. As skills-based hiring has spread to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers</a>, more of them are willing to look at the ability directly, through projects, take-home tasks, and skills tests. The credential helps, but it is no longer the only key.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Which tech roles are most open without a CS degree?</h2>
      <p>Aim where the door is already open. Product management rewards clear thinking about problems and trade-offs. Product and UX design reward taste and craft you can show. Growth and marketing reward judgment about audiences and messages. Data analysis rewards the ability to turn numbers into a recommendation. Technical sales, customer success, and operations reward communication and execution. In every one of these, a hiring manager would rather see a strong piece of relevant work than confirm which department issued your degree. Pick the one that fits how you think, and you are no longer fighting the credential at all.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you prove you can do the job without the degree?</h2>
      <p>Replace the credential with evidence. Do a real task from the role and get it evaluated against a professional standard, so its quality is established rather than claimed. A product spec, a design, a growth plan, a data analysis that ends in a recommendation. The goal is a small body of work that lets a recruiter judge you on what you can produce, which is the one comparison where not having a CS degree stops mattering. Our guide on <a href="/learn/how-do-you-prove-you-can-do-the-job-before-you-have-the-job">proving you can do the job</a> walks through what that evidence looks like in detail.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Bootcamp, course, or scored simulator?</h2>
      <p>Judge every option by one test: does it leave you with proof a recruiter will actually evaluate, or just a certificate. Courses and bootcamps can genuinely teach skills, and some are excellent, but many end in a completion badge that carries the same weak signal as any other line on a résumé. The thing you want is evaluated work in your target role that recruiters can see. zero is built for exactly that: you take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against the bar professionals are held to, and the scored result becomes a receipt recruiters can evaluate. It is free for students because recruiters pay for the access. Whatever path you choose, optimize for the proof, not the badge.</p>
    </section>

